WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.830
The Long Island Horticultural Research
and Extension Center is a campus of

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:10.620
Cornell University. We've been here in
Riverhead, New York for 98 years and our

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.160
purpose here is to help the agricultural
industry

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:22.140
succeed. There is a lot of need for
up-to-date research and assistance with

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.000
growing plants. The impatiens is
primarily of interest to the greenhouse

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.260
growers on Long Island as well as some
of the nurseries -- the commercial, the

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.700
retail establishments that sell plants.
Right here we've been breeding impatiens

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.989
plants trying to develop
resistance to the disease called

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:46.020
impatiens downy mildew. And there's
always problems when you do breeding and

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.100
one of the one of the issues that we
found out with this plant is they have

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.270
what we call explosive seed pods so that
means when the seed pods are ripe they explode.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.020
And of course if you're trying to
harvest seed that's a problem because

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.949
then they're all over the greenhouse
floor. So we use we use a technique

00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:06.450
called plant tissue culture to assist
our breeding program and essentially

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.820
plant tissue culture is a way to grow
plants in a sterile environment on a

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.170
nutrient medium under controlled
environment. So we will remove the pods

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:22.409
that we've pollinated on these plants
about 14 to 16 days after we've

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:27.299
pollinated them and we take those pods
to our laboratory they're sterilized on

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:31.829
the outside and then they're opened
aseptic ly to expose the seeds and those

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:36.600
seeds that are placed in tissue culture
to germinate. The nice thing about that

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.380
process is that once they germinate
in tissue culture they're always going

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.460
to be there as a stock plant for us.
They're always going be available. We can

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.610
take cuttings of those plants and bring
them back here to the greenhouse. If we

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:52.200
find out that we have a successful plant,
a successful hybrid, we have the mother

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.390
plant in tissue culture free of diseases
free of insects and we can then rapidly

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:02.760
propagate that plant to increase the
numbers quickly.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.820
And these are the results of some of
that breeding research. So all these

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.810
plants that you see here are resistant
to the disease. We've done field trials

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:20.250
for them for the last two years but our
next step then is to produce a plant

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:26.880
that has a nice looking flower and a
nice symmetrical growth habit so we are

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:34.680
continuing to hybridize these plants and
will continue to develop it until we

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.540
get a nice seed propagated plant.

